# The shop's configuration on a server (Phase 8, lesson 3). Copy it to the server as .env
# (cp .env.production.example .env), change the values and don't push it to Git: .env is in .gitignore.
#   chmod 600 .env    so only your user reads it
PORT=3000
LOG_LEVEL=info
# #region secrets
# The secret the shop shares with the payment gateway to sign the webhooks. The server doesn't start
# with this value, or with one under 32 characters. Generate it on the server and paste it here:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
WEBHOOK_SECRET=change-this-to-a-secret-from-openssl-rand
# The password of the database Compose creates. Empty, docker compose doesn't start. It goes inside
# a URL, so only letters and numbers. Generate it on the server:
#   openssl rand -hex 24
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# #endregion secrets
# The sites requests with the session cookie are accepted from: the shop itself, with https and
# with your domain's name (with the free path, your server's sslip.io name). Change it to yours.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://shop.example
# Only for development: names the shop can send notifications to over http. On a server, empty.
NOTIFICATION_DEV_HOSTS=
# The address the API sees nginx's requests arrive from: the gateway of Compose's network, pinned in
# compose.production.yaml (172.18.0.0/16). The visitor's IP is only read from X-Forwarded-For when
# the connection comes from it. Only ONE proxy hop is supported (the last entry of that list): with
# another proxy in front (Cloudflare's, say), the limiter would see that proxy's addresses. Check it with:
#   docker network inspect shop_default --format '{{(index .IPAM.Config 0).Gateway}}'
TRUSTED_PROXY=172.18.0.1
# #region email
# The mail server (Phase 9), for the confirmation email of a paid order. Without it, the shop works
# all the same and sends no emails: the log says which one would have gone out. With an email
# provider (it gives you the server, the user and the password), remove the # from the two lines
# and put yours, and the sender, an address on your domain. So the emails don't end up in spam, the
# provider will ask you for some records in your domain's DNS (SPF and DKIM). The password goes
# inside a URL: a symbol is written with its code (an @, %40).
# SMTP_URL=smtps://user:password@smtp.provider.example:465
# SMTP_FROM=The shop <orders@shop.example>
# #endregion email
# The catalogue's cache (Phase 9) isn't configured here: compose.production.yaml gives the API
# CACHE_URL=redis://cache:6379, the cache service. Without that service, remove it from there and
# the shop always reads from PostgreSQL.
# The API's image. Without this, the one docker compose up --build builds (shop-api). With lesson
# 6's automatic publishing, the one from ghcr.io: ghcr.io/<your-user>/shop-api:main
# IMAGE=ghcr.io/your-user/shop-api:main
# #region provider
# The identity provider (Phase 6, lesson 4): leave it like this and the shop works with email and
# password. To offer "Sign in with Google" (or another provider), remove the # from the four lines:
# either all four are there or none (an empty one makes the server not start). The callback URL
# has to be https and on a domain of yours: Google doesn't accept an IP or sslip.io.
# OIDC_ISSUER=https://accounts.google.com
# OIDC_CLIENT_ID=the-id-google-gives-you
# OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=the-secret-google-gives-you
# OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=https://shop.example/v2/session/provider/callback
# #endregion provider
# Whether the API checks its own responses against the contract (Phase 10, lesson 3): no, log (a
# warning in the log for each response that doesn't meet it) or fail (a 500 instead). It costs
# time on every response: measure it before turning it on (npm run load).
VALIDATE_RESPONSES=no
